window.docContents[41].resolve({"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates":{"contents":"PDE arguments repeatedly move between three coordinate languages. Evolution\nequations use a canonical point of  with time stored in\ncoordinate zero. Radial differential identities replace\n by a one-variable profile. Polar integration\nreplaces Euclidean volume by an angular measure and a radial measure carrying\nthe Jacobian. These are different transformations with different hypotheses,\nso each has its own interface.\n\nThe spacetime convention is shared by the heat, wave, transport,\nconservation-law, Hamilton–Jacobi, and fluid equations.\nIf , then\n\n\n\nThe canonical vector therefore looks like  at the coordinate level.\nHowever, many analytic APIs curry a field as  or use the product\n. Explicit round trips convert between the\norders. In particular,\nsplitSpaceTime returns , joinSpaceTime accepts , and the\nseparate timeSpaceContinuousLinearEquiv exposes the order .\n\nFor derivatives, the central statement identifies the Euclidean Laplacian of\na radial field with\n away from the origin. The logarithmic and power\nprofiles then supply the calculations used by Laplace and Poisson fundamental\nsolutions.\n\nFor integration, the formal route is measure theoretic. A\nmeasure-preserving equivalence gives the coordinate change, and Fubini turns\nit into the familiar iterated polar formula with the Jacobian already encoded\nin the radial measure.\nThe radial Jacobian decides whether a singularity is integrable. In positive\ndimension, a shell of radius  and thickness  has volume\n. Thus, for the nonnegative radial function\n on the unit ball,\n\n\n\nThis is finite exactly when ; in that case its value is\n. At  the radial integral is\n, which diverges logarithmically. The value assigned at the\nsingle point  does not change the integral. This power count determines whether a singular fundamental solution is\nlocally integrable at its source.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus","header":"3.3.1. Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Vorticity":{"contents":"For a three-dimensional velocity field, set .\nTaking the curl of the unforced Euler equation yields\n\n\n\nThe right-hand side stretches and tilts vortex lines. In two dimensions\nvorticity is scalar and this term disappears:\n\n\n\nThis difference is one reason two-dimensional incompressible flow is more\nregular than its three-dimensional counterpart.\n\nFor a flow in  space dimensions, define the antisymmetric\nvelocity-gradient componentsFor a planar spacetime field, scalar vorticity is\n.\n\nAntisymmetry is what makes the planar case scalar. An antisymmetric \nmatrix has a single independent entry, so in two dimensions the whole tensor is\ndetermined by , while in three dimensions its three\nindependent entries are, up to sign, the components of .\n\nThe vorticity tensor is antisymmetric:\n\nOn the diagonal the two derivatives are identical and cancel. Exchanging\n and  reverses the order of the same difference, hence changes its\nsign.\n\nWith vorticity scalar in the plane, the stretching term \nof the three-dimensional equation has no counterpart: the vorticity vector is\nnormal to the plane of motion and the velocity does not vary in that direction.\nWhat remains is pure transport, and its residual is the material derivative of\nthe scalar .\n\nFor planar flow, the vorticity-transport residual isFor unforced planar Euler flow, the vorticity equation is\n.\n\nThe residual is a definition; the content is that for solutions of the momentum\nequation it equals the curl of the force, hence vanishes in the unforced case.\nTaking the planar curl of  eliminates\nthe pressure, because the curl of a gradient is zero, and incompressibility\nconverts the curl of the convective term into transport of . Since the\nderivation differentiates the momentum equation, it needs that equation on an\nopen neighborhood rather than at a single point.\n\nLet  be jointly  in space and time, let the pressure slice be\n, and let the force slice be . Suppose the momentum equation and\n hold on an open spatial neighborhood of . For standard\ncounterclockwise vorticity ,The clockwise-sign counterpart\n, used by the\nBoussinesq system, satisfies the same identity with the clockwise curl of the\nforce on the right; the two statements differ by an overall sign, and the\nauxiliary commutation identities are proved in both conventions. In\nparticular, either right-hand side vanishes for unforced planar Euler flow.\n\nDifferentiate the two momentum components in the transverse spatial\ndirections and subtract. Joint  regularity commutes the time and spatial\nderivatives. The pressure contribution vanishes because mixed derivatives\ncommute. For a divergence-free planar velocity, the clockwise curl of\n is . What remains is exactly\nthe clockwise curl of the force. Lean proves the clockwise form first;\nnegating it gives the counterclockwise statement.\n\nIn three dimensions the curl of the convective term is not pure transport: the\nadditional term  survives and is responsible for vortex\nstretching. The general residual keeps that term and includes a viscous term,\nso Euler and Navier–Stokes flow share it.\n\nFor spacetime fields , with time in\ncoordinate zero, the vortex-stretching term is the convective derivative of\nthe velocity along ,and the vorticity transport residual with viscosity  is the vectorWhen , , and  is divergence free,\n is the unforced Euler vorticity equation and\n with  is the Navier–Stokes vorticity equation.\nIn the definition  is an independent vector field in any dimension;\nfor Navier–Stokes flow its identification with the curl and with the two-form\nvorticity equation is a theorem.\n\nThe residual vanishes exactly whenand at zero viscosity it reduces to the transport-stretching difference\n.\n\nBoth statements are rearrangements in the additive group of vector fields:\n holds exactly when , and the scalar multiple\n vanishes.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.4. Vorticity","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Vorticity"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--How-the-subject-acquired-its-language":{"contents":"The higher heat modes suggest a way to handle more general initial data:\nexpand the temperature in a sine series and evolve each coefficient. For a\nfinite sum, differentiation and addition commute and the calculation is\nimmediate. For an infinite series, convergence, differentiation, and recovery\nof the initial function become part of the problem. This is an old source of\nnew analysis.\n\nD'Alembert's wave equation already raised the question: its solutions involve\narbitrary traveling profiles, while the eighteenth-century understanding of a\nfunction was tied to analytic expressions, and Euler, d'Alembert, and the\nBernoullis disagreed over which shapes could count as initial data. Fourier's\ntrigonometric series represented rough initial temperatures and evolved them\nby damping high frequencies long before convergence and termwise\ndifferentiation were understood. These two equations pushed analysis toward\nthe arbitrary function, rigorous integration, and Fourier analysis.\n\nPotential theory exposed a different difficulty. Dirichlet's principle\nrelated a harmonic function with prescribed boundary values to a minimizer of\nan energy. Riemann used the principle successfully, but Weierstrass objected\nto the existence argument: an energy can have an infimum without attaining\nit. Choosing functions whose energies approach the best possible value does\nnot yet produce a best function. Completeness, compactness, and lower\nsemicontinuity explain when that limiting step works. We will meet the same\nissue when constructing weak solutions from approximations.\n\nHilbert-space methods gave linear PDE an abstract setting, but limits still\nneed not preserve classical derivatives. Sobolev's weak derivatives made it\npossible to complete smooth functions in norms controlling both values and\nderivatives. Schwartz's distributions gave differentiation a setting in which\nit is always defined and continuous. Leray's Navier–Stokes construction\ncombined a weak formulation with energy control to obtain global solutions,\nwithout thereby obtaining global smoothness or uniqueness. This remains a\nproductive way to approach an equation: choose a solution concept stable\nunder the estimates, construct a solution there, and then investigate its\nregularity. Entropy and viscosity solutions make different choices because\nshocks and nondifferentiable value functions present different obstacles.\n\nComputer-assisted analysis creates another need for a usable mathematical\nobject. We may be able to inspect the idea of an estimate without reading\nevery one of its arithmetic steps. A certificate records data from which a\nchecker can prove the claimed identity or bound. The search for it can use\nheuristics; the accepted result has a precise mathematical meaning. PDEAtlas\nuses such certificates alongside weak derivatives and energy estimates, so\nthat a long calculation can enter an argument through an ordinary theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.2. How the subject acquired its language","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--How-the-subject-acquired-its-language"}});